@host = https://localhost:7097

### 1. Simulate an external subscriber: CRM service wants every user event
POST {{host}}/integration-events/subscriptions
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "queueName": "crm-service-users",
  "bindingKey": "integration.user.*"
}

### 2. Simulate another external subscriber: audit service wants EVERYTHING
POST {{host}}/integration-events/subscriptions
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "queueName": "audit-service-firehose",
  "bindingKey": "integration.#"
}

### 3. Publish a user event → routed to BOTH queues (crm matches integration.user.*, audit matches integration.#)
POST {{host}}/integration-events
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "domain": "user",
  "action": "created",
  "data": "{\"userId\": 42, \"email\": \"jane@example.com\"}"
}

### 4. Publish a booking event → routed ONLY to the audit queue (crm's pattern does not match)
POST {{host}}/integration-events
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "domain": "booking",
  "action": "cancelled",
  "data": "{\"bookingId\": \"B-1001\"}"
}

### 5. Verify routing: crm queue should hold 1 message
GET {{host}}/integration-events/subscriptions/crm-service-users

### 6. Verify routing: audit queue should hold 2 messages
GET {{host}}/integration-events/subscriptions/audit-service-firehose

### 7. Run this BEFORE creating any subscription: publishing still succeeds — with no matching
###    binding the broker simply drops the message (topic semantics). The publisher never fails
###    because subscribers are missing; the exchange always exists thanks to DeclareExchange.
POST {{host}}/integration-events
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "domain": "inventory",
  "action": "restocked",
  "data": null
}
